1. Authentication vs Authorization

These two words are easy to confuse.

Authentication

"Who are you?"

For example:

Email: manager@gmail.com
Password: ********

The system checks:

Is this really the manager?

If yes:

✅ Authentication successful
Authorization

"What are you allowed to do?"

Suppose the user is authenticated as:

Manager

The system asks:

Can this person delete users?

Maybe:

❌ No

But:

Can this person view stock?

✅ Yes

So remember:

Authentication
     ↓
Who are you?

Authorization
     ↓
What can you do?
2. Real-world example

Imagine ASTU Stock has three employees:

👑 Admin
👨‍💼 Manager
📦 Storekeeper

They all have accounts.

They can all potentially log in.

But they don't necessarily have the same permissions.

For example:

Action	Admin	Manager	Storekeeper
View products	✅	✅	✅
Add products	✅	✅	✅
Edit products	✅	✅	✅
Delete products	✅	Maybe	❌
Manage users	✅	❌	❌
View reports	✅	✅	Maybe
Manage suppliers	✅	✅	✅

Important: This is an example for practice. Your actual permissions should ultimately come from your SRS/business requirements.

